WEBVTT

00:00:06.988 --> 00:00:14.665
<v Jamie>CPI just dropped, and the market's response is to dump Google, Oracle, and Adobe—which tracks if you believe good news is actually a personal betrayal.

00:00:14.665 --> 00:00:45.667
<v Alex>So yesterday we watched an Israeli defense contractor post record earnings and the stock got buried anyway—the Iran premium at work. Today that pattern is everywhere. CPI falls, which should be champagne-and-fireworks material, except the market reads it as permission to short Nvidia and Adobe because suddenly the Fed doesn't have to stay locked in at restrictive rates. Geopolitical risk and rate expectations are repricing simultaneously, and they're moving in opposite directions. The defense play that should have sailed is sinking because capital's rotating out of growth into hedges. Oil jumped on the news. That's the real story underneath all the individual stock carnage.
  ↳ source: https://www.investors.com/market-trend/stock-market-today/dow-jones-sp500-nasdaq-july-cpi-inflation-nvidia-partner-coreweave/?src=A00220&yptr=yahoo

00:00:45.667 --> 00:01:10.173
<v Alex>S&P down point three two percent, Nasdaq down point six, but the real fracture is in the winners and losers. Home Depot up one point zero five, AMD up one point zero one—materials and chips that actually build things are catching bids. Meanwhile Google down three point eight four percent, Oracle down three point six nine, Adobe down three point three nine. The pattern is: anything that looked expensive on growth assumptions gets sold the moment the Fed has permission to cut.

00:01:10.173 --> 00:01:18.440
<v Jamie>So the CPI number we all wanted is the exact trigger that makes the market punish the companies that would have benefited most from a rate cut.

00:01:18.440 --> 00:01:34.680
<v Maya>It's like someone finally fixed the WiFi and the first thing everyone does is download a virus—Google's down nearly four percent because inflation cooling means the Fed isn't forced to be their financial life support anymore, and suddenly nobody wants to own a stock trading at high multiples when Treasury yields are actually respectable.

00:01:34.680 --> 00:01:48.557
<v Alex>Gold up, the dollar barely budged. That's the real tell. Investors aren't running toward the dollar or away from it. They're rotating into hard assets and out of duration risk. Berkshire down two point four six, Amazon down two point zero nine—the mega-cap narrative is breaking.

00:01:48.557 --> 00:01:55.052
<v Jamie>So good news came in, and the market's response was to remind big tech that the free money era actually ended.

00:01:55.052 --> 00:02:10.406
<v Alex>The mechanism here is almost mechanical. CPI came in cooler than expected, which gives the Fed actual room to pause, maybe even cut. But the moment that number hit the tape, the math changed on every mega-cap valuation that was built on the assumption the Fed stays in crisis mode forever.

00:02:10.406 --> 00:02:21.626
<v Maya>Right, but like—inflation cooling should be the thing that makes investors feel safe enough to own expensive growth stocks again. Instead we're watching them bail on Google like the company just announced it's pivoting to something else.

00:02:21.626 --> 00:02:39.636
<v Alex>That's the inversion nobody expected. When the Fed is tightening, expensive stocks with long-duration cash flows are getting murdered anyway, so at least you own them hoping for relief. The moment relief looks real, those same investors ask: why am I paying high multiples for Google when a Treasury bond now yields higher and actually gets me paid to wait?

00:02:39.636 --> 00:02:49.084
<v Jamie>So the pivot narrative that's been the entire foundation of the mega-cap rally—the idea that the Fed would have to cut because inflation was sticky—that just evaporated in one CPI print.

00:02:49.084 --> 00:03:02.962
<v Maya>And what's wild is the folks who were screaming for this exact number are now watching their portfolios crater because they built their entire thesis on the Fed being forced to capitulate. It's like ordering a rescue helicopter and then panicking when it actually shows up.

00:03:02.962 --> 00:03:14.772
<v Alex>The real question now is whether this holds. If we get another print like this, the Fed's actually got optionality—they don't have to cut. And if they don't have to cut, the entire repricing of growth equities is just beginning.

00:03:14.772 --> 00:03:21.563
<v Jamie>So the mega-cap narrative is cracking, but the actual capex cycle—the infrastructure companies that have to build the pipes—those are still real.

00:03:21.563 --> 00:03:29.535
<v Alex>CoreWeave's revenue jumped significantly year-over-year. The Fed cutting rates doesn't kill demand for GPUs and cooling systems. It just kills the multiple you'll pay for Google.

00:03:29.535 --> 00:03:33.964
<v Maya>Tencent just beat on games and AI-driven advertising revenue while we're watching Google crater.

00:03:33.964 --> 00:03:40.459
<v Jamie>Wait, so Tencent's actually executing on AI monetization while the American mega-caps are getting punished for being too expensive to own?

00:03:40.459 --> 00:03:47.545
<v Maya>Their ad platform is using AI to target users in ways that make our regulatory theater look like a elementary school bake sale.

00:03:47.545 --> 00:03:54.927
<v Jamie>Sonos just filed new headphones with the FCC—the company that nearly torched its own app last year is betting it can sell you hardware again.

00:03:54.927 --> 00:04:03.489
<v Maya>They're coming out with new headphones. This is what happens when your software update becomes a meme—you pivot back to the thing you can't break over the internet.

00:04:03.489 --> 00:04:10.280
<v Alex>Europe's nuclear plants are shutting down because cooling water is too hot. The energy transition just hit a wall made of climate.

00:04:10.280 --> 00:04:18.548
<v Jamie>So we built nuclear plants assuming water would stay cold, and now the water's too hot to cool the plants that were supposed to solve climate change.

00:04:18.548 --> 00:04:33.015
<v Maya>The UK Prime Minister just said disposable BBQs shouldn't be used right now because people keep leaving them burning on beaches and in parks. We're not talking about a climate regulation here—we're talking about the government having to step in because humans can't be trusted with a grill.

00:04:33.015 --> 00:04:41.873
<v Jamie>Wait, so the problem isn't that disposable BBQs exist—it's that people are using them correctly, then just walking away and leaving a live charcoal fire in a public space?

00:04:41.873 --> 00:04:56.931
<v Alex>Wildfire season is real now. But yes, the mechanism of failure here is that a disposable grill is so cheap and lightweight that the barrier to just abandoning it is basically zero. It's not regulatory overreach—it's the market finally pricing in the cost of letting people treat fire as single-use.

00:04:56.931 --> 00:05:05.789
<v Jamie>On the tape tomorrow, we've got a cluster of biotech and aerospace earnings hitting at the same time—Applied Aerospace, Acumen Pharmaceuticals, Arbutus Biopharma, Altimmune, Annexon, and Aprea all reporting.

00:05:05.789 --> 00:05:19.666
<v Alex>That's a dump, which means the market's going to have to pick a lane fast. Biotech's been getting crushed on higher rates, and aerospace is riding the defense bid we talked about earlier—so you've got two entirely different risk regimes colliding in the same earnings cycle.

00:05:19.666 --> 00:05:32.657
<v Maya>Applied Aerospace is the one to watch because it's literally the supply chain for the defense contractors that are actually printing money right now. If they miss, that whole narrative about capex moving to hard infrastructure instead of software gets a reality check.

00:05:32.657 --> 00:05:38.858
<v Maya>Google spent the day paying for the privilege of being too expensive to own when bonds finally became worth holding.

00:05:38.858 --> 00:05:43.582
<v Alex>Which means the real winners tomorrow are the companies that still need to build something.

00:05:43.582 --> 00:05:47.715
<v Jamie>This show is for entertainment and education only — nothing here is investment advice.
